                <h2>Why Ad Agency Account Opening Needs Kanban Management</h2>
<p>In overseas ad agency account opening services, operations teams frequently encounter these challenges: client documents scattered across WeChat, emails, and cloud storage; account opening processes stuck at certain stages for over a week with no clear reason; discrepancies between top-up amounts and account balances during month-end reconciliation. Kanban management transforms these chaotic processes into clearly visible nodes, giving every stage a responsible person, time limits, and alerts.</p>
<h2>6 Steps to Build Your Kanban System</h2>
<h3>Step 1: Break Down the Opening Process</h3>
<p>Don't pursue perfection from the start. First, split the opening process into 5-7 clear stages:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Initiation</strong>: Initial client qualification screening, account type confirmation</li>
<li><strong>Documentation</strong>: Business license, domain verification, BM creation, etc.</li>
<li><strong>Qualification Review</strong>: Platform policy check, account permission application</li>
<li><strong>Top-up &amp; Deposit</strong>: Amount confirmation, top-up submission, receipt verification</li>
<li><strong>Account Activation</strong>: Pixel installation, domain verification, ad authorization</li>
<li><strong>Delivery Risk Control</strong>: First-day delivery monitoring, budget adjustment confirmation</li>
</ul>
<h3>Step 2: Design Columns and Flow Rules</h3>
<p>Each stage corresponds to one column, with flow rules defined within columns:</p>
<ul>
<li>What conditions allow moving to the next column</li>
<li>What situations should be marked as "exception"</li>
<li>What situations can be "temporarily paused"</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Tip</strong>: Use colors to distinguish platforms (Meta in blue, Google in green, TikTok in purple) for easy filtering by business line.</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>Step 3: Design Card Information</h3>
<p>Each account opening card should contain these key details:</p>
<ul>
<li>Client name and contact information</li>
<li>Platform and account type</li>
<li>Submission time and estimated completion time</li>
<li>Required documents checklist and current missing items</li>
<li>Responsible person</li>
</ul>
<h3>Step 4: Set WIP Limits</h3>
<p>WIP (Work In Progress) limits are the core mechanism of Kanban. No more than 5-8 account opening tasks should run simultaneously per stage. When exceeded, implement alerts or pause new task entry.</p>
<h3>Step 5: Establish Exception Alert Rules</h3>
<p>Set reasonable durations for each stage based on historical data:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Stage</th>
<th>Normal Duration</th>
<th>Alert Threshold</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Documentation</td>
<td>1-2 days</td>
<td>Over 3 days</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Qualification Review</td>
<td>1-3 days</td>
<td>Over 5 days</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Top-up Receipt</td>
<td>1-2 days</td>
<td>Over 24 hours unconfirmed</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Account Activation</td>
<td>1 day</td>
<td>Over 2 days</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Step 6: Regular Review and Optimization</h3>
<p>Weekly Kanban data review: Which columns have the most backlog? Which exceptions recur? Which steps can be merged or removed?</p>
<h2>Account Opening Process Checklist</h2>
<h3>Initiation Stage Checklist</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Client qualification meets platform minimum requirements</li>
<li>[ ] Confirmed whether opening personal or business account</li>
<li>[ ] Document checklist sent to client and receipt confirmed</li>
</ul>
<h3>Documentation Review Stage Checklist</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Business license legal person matches website ICP filing</li>
<li>[ ] Website content matches account opening category</li>
<li>[ ] Social media page links are authentic and accessible</li>
<li>[ ] Domain ownership verification completed</li>
</ul>
<h3>Top-up Confirmation Stage Checklist</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Top-up amount matches client prepayment amount</li>
<li>[ ] Screenshot archived and account ID registered</li>
<li>[ ] Account balance confirmed updated</li>
<li>[ ] Top-up receipt sent to client</li>
</ul>
<h3>Risk Control Monitoring Stage Checklist</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Ad content complies with platform policies</li>
<li>[ ] No account suspension on first day</li>
<li>[ ] Budget consumption matches expectations</li>
<li>[ ] Client confirmed ad campaign configuration</li>
</ul>
<h2>FAQ</h2>
<p><strong>Q: How to manage multi-platform account openings in parallel?</strong></p>
<p>Recommend grouping Kanban by platform, with separate WIP limits for each group. Alternatively, use Swimlane mode with platforms on the horizontal axis and process stages on the vertical axis.</p>
<p><strong>Q: What to do when client contact changes cause information gaps?</strong></p>
<p>Record key communication milestones in card notes. Preferably keep written records in WeChat Work or email rather than relying on verbal commitments.</p>
<p><strong>Q: How to handle discrepancies found after top-up?</strong></p>
<p>Immediately screenshot current account balance as evidence, contact platform customer service to confirm top-up records, and reassure the client while informing them of the handling timeline.</p>
<h2>Summary</h2>
<p>The value of Kanban management lies in transforming "not knowing where it's stuck" into "seeing where it's stuck at a glance." For teams handling more than 5 account openings daily, a Kanban system can reduce average opening cycle time by over 40% and complaints by 30%. The key is to start running first without pursuing perfection from the start. Use simple spreadsheet tools to practice initially, then gradually upgrade to professional Kanban software.</p>
